    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nWith `cudagraph_mm_encoder` on and video enabled, Gemma 4 gives every encoder CUDA graph a pixel buffer of `max(max_batch_size, max_frames_per_batch)` full-image rows. With the auto-inferred sizes that is 1856 rows at `--max-num-batched-tokens 4096`, and capture OOMs. This PR caps the rows at `token_budget // 70`, which is the most rows a replay of that budget can hold.\n\nFixes #61045.\n\n## Claims\n\n- `google/gemma-4-E2B-it` with `cudagraph_mm_encoder` and video enabled now starts on a 48 GB L40S. On main it OOMs during capture with `Tried to allocate 6.69 GiB`, the same error as in #61045.\n- Capture rows for budgets 70 to 4096 go from 1856 each to 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 and 58.\n- Replay is unchanged. A budget-B replay never has more than B // 70 rows, because each image bills `max_soft_tokens` (at least 70) and each video frame bills 70. Image outputs match eager up to the bf16 noise eager itself shows between runs.\n- The CI config (`max_frames_per_batch=16`, budget `[1120]`) still gets 16 rows.\n\n## Validation\n\nSetup: 1xL40S, nightly `2eb491e18b` with either main (`239a3fa3a8`) or this branch overlaid. All runs use `LLM(model=\"google/gemma-4-E2B-it\", max_model_len=8192, max_num_batched_tokens=4096, max_num_seqs=8, gpu_memory_utilization=0.85)` with greedy decoding for 48 tokens. Inputs are 4 images (stop_sign, cherry_blossom, and a resized copy of each), run batched and one by one, plus a 16-frame `baby_reading` video.\n\n| encoder | `limit_mm_per_prompt` | main | this PR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CG | image=1, video=1 (`max_frames_per_batch=1856`) | OOM in capture: `Tried to allocate 6.69 GiB` | starts; 7 graphs; all 5 items replayed through graphs (5 hits, 0 misses) |\n| CG | image=1, video=0 (`max_frames_per_batch=0`) | starts; 58 rows for every budget | starts; 1 to 58 rows |\n| eager | image=1, video=1 | ok | ok |\n\nGreedy token ids compared with eager:\n\n- **Images:** identical, except cherry_blossom, which switches between two continuations at token 6 to 18. Eager does the same between the main and branch runs, and so does image-only CG on main.\n- **Video:** CG differs from token 0, on this branch and on main whenever video CG runs (for example with an explicit `max_frames_per_batch`). That is the replay gather stride mismatch #60687 fixes. With #60687 on top of this PR, the video output is `A baby is reading a book while wearing glasses.` vs eager `A baby is shown reading a book while wearing glasses.` (first diff at token 3).\n\nExisting test:\n\n```bash\npytest tests/models/multimodal/generation/test_vit_cudagraph.py -k gemma4\n```\n\nOn 1xL40S:\n\n- `test_vit_cudagraph_image[gemma4]` passes on this branch.\n- When `test_vit_cudagraph_video[gemma4]` runs right after the image test in the same session, it segfaults in a CPU op during engine init, before encoder capture. Main does the same, with the same stack.\n- Run on its own, the video test passes on both main and this branch. This config pins 16 rows, and this PR keeps 16.\n\n`pre-commit run --files vllm/model_executor/models/gemma4_mm.py` passes.\n\n## Details\n\nRoot cause: when nothing is set, `EncoderCudaGraphManager` infers `max_batch_size = min(4096 // 70, 70) = 58` and, with video enabled, `max_frames_per_batch = 58 * 32 = 1856`. Gemma 4's `prepare_encoder_cudagraph_capture_inputs` uses `max(max_batch_size, max_frames_per_batch)` as the row count of a dense `[rows, min(budget, 280) * 9, 768]` buffer. So every budget of 280 or more holds a `(1856, 2520, 768)` bf16 dummy (6.69 GiB), which stays alive as the graph's input buffer. The warm-up forward also pushes about 4.7M patch tokens through the vision tower in one call.\n\n`_execute_local` bills each row (an image or a frame) at least 70 output tokens and replays a batch on the smallest budget that covers its total. So `token_budget // 70` rows always fit. The outer `max(1, ...)` keeps user-set budgets below 70 capturable as before. Row width is unchanged: video frames still get full-image-width rows. Separate per-modality graphs would save more, but that is a bigger change.\n\nNo test added: the gemma4 entry in `test_vit_cudagraph.py` pins the sizes on purpose to keep CI memory low. Covering auto sizing there would mean capturing all default budgets in CI.\n\nNot a duplicate: no PR references #61045 (searched by number and by \"gemma4 encoder cudagraph\", \"max_frames_per_batch\" and \"prepare_encoder_cudagraph_capture_inputs\"). #60687 changes the same function (the `max_soft_tokens` lookup and the gather stride) but not the row count, so the two are complementary. #60112 adds an eager fallback when a replay exceeds the captured buffers, which leaves the capture size unchanged.\n\nAI assistance (Claude Code) was used to trace the sizing, write the change and run the validation above.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
